12:A workspace holds tabs. A tab holds panes.
15:Workspace
20:### Workspace
22:One project folder. In most cases, the folder is one repository. A workspace holds tabs.
26:One entry of the tab bar. A tab belongs to one workspace. A tab holds one or more panes.
36:The horizontal row at the top of the viewport, inside the border of the viewport. It shows the tabs of the selected workspace, one entry for each tab.
44:A pane that the developer keeps visible. The pin button in the header of the pane pins the pane. A pinned pane stays visible, with the same width, when the developer changes tab or workspace.
60:One part of a sidebar, for example the Needs you, the Scripts, the Source control, or the Files sidebar section. A sidebar holds several sidebar sections, and shows one sidebar section at a time.
64:The sidebar section of the left sidebar that shows the Git changes of the selected workspace, as the source control view of VS Code does. From the top: a header with the title "Source Control" and its buttons, the commit message box, the commit button, the group "Staged Changes", and the group "Changes". It shows only the local Git repository, nothing of GitHub or GitLab. It replaces the Git sidebar section.
68:The sidebar section of the left sidebar that shows what lives on GitHub or on GitLab for the repository of the selected workspace: a list of the open issues, and a list of the open pull requests (GitHub) or merge requests (GitLab). A row has three lines and no button. A click on a row opens an information card. The words for its search, such as "saved search", are in [glossary_code_hosting.md](glossary_code_hosting.md).

